{ Tests for the journal thread (AMQP.Server.Journal) -- WS2 of Phase 4
  (durability, see CLAUDE.md, decisions D19-D28).

  WHAT THIS SUITE MUST PROVE, and that is not obvious:

  1. That group commit GROUPS. "Wrote everything" is not enough: all of D25 is the
     bet that the batch grows by itself under load, and the number that shows it
     is Records/Syncs. Without this measure, an implementation that does one fsync
     per record would pass every other test.

  2. That the batch is INDIVISIBLE. Four threads submitting pairs (aN, bN) in one
     call each: in the file the pairs must come out ADJACENT and with consecutive
     LSNs. Without this check, assigning the LSNs under the lock and enqueueing
     OUTSIDE it would go unnoticed -- that was exactly the mutation that motivated the
     test. (It is caught twice, by the way: WS1's increasing-LSN invariant of the
     segment rejects the interleaved write at the file layer.)

  3. That the watermark does NOT move when the fsync fails. It is the difference between
     promising durability and lying about it, and it is only observable with the file
     test double (layer 2 of D28).

  4. That a WRITE failure closes the door for good, while an FSYNC failure
     is recoverable. They are deliberately different treatments: in the
     first case the record is nowhere; in the second the bytes are already
     in the file and a later fsync covers them.

  The file test double (TAMQPFakeWalFile) lives in AMQP.ServerTestDoubles, together
  with the delivery target -- same unit and same reason: two test files
  need the same object, and duplicated scaffolding is the surface where this
  codebase has already gone wrong (fixing one side and forgetting the twin).

  DUnitX master in FPCUnit's dialect (TAssert.*); the FPCUnit mirror in
  fpc\ is generated by tools/gen_fpc_mirror.py. }


